= Intersubjectivity =

'''Intersubjectivity''' concerns relations among subjects through which other minds, experiences, meanings, intentions, and aspects of a shared world become mutually intelligible. The term is used in phenomenology, social theory, developmental psychology, linguistics, interaction research, and cognitive science, but these traditions do not all mean exactly the same thing.

At minimum, intersubjectivity involves more than two individuals merely existing near one another. It concerns some relation between perspectives: recognizing another as a subject, coordinating attention, establishing shared reference, negotiating meaning, or participating in practices treated as mutually intelligible.

Agreement is not required. Two people can understand one another perfectly and still disagree. Likewise, agreement does not prove intersubjective understanding; people can conform, repeat the same error, or coordinate behavior without sharing the same interpretation.

== Philosophical background ==

In phenomenology, intersubjectivity addresses how a world experienced from a first-person perspective can also be understood as available to other subjects.

Edmund Husserl treated the experience of others, empathy, embodiment, and the constitution of an objective shared world as central problems of phenomenology.<ref name="husserl">[https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/husserl/ Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Edmund Husserl.”]</ref>

The problem is not simply whether another body is visible. It is how another embodied perspective is experienced as another subject rather than merely an object.

Later phenomenological and social traditions developed different accounts of reciprocity, sociality, language, and shared meaning.

== Shared world ==

Intersubjectivity is often connected to the possibility of an objective or shared world.

If an object can be encountered from multiple perspectives, no one perspective exhausts it.

A table can appear differently from opposite sides while still being treated as the same object.

The shared-world problem therefore involves:

* perspectival difference;
* coordination of reference;
* expectation that others can encounter the same world;
* correction when perspectives conflict.

This philosophical question is broader than experimental measurement of agreement.

== Perspective relation ==

A useful minimal representation is:

<pre>
subject A → object or situation ← subject B
      ↘ interaction / interpretation ↙
</pre>

The participants need not have identical internal states.

What matters is that their perspectives become related through attention, communication, action, convention, or mutual responsiveness.

Some contemporary dialogical approaches therefore treat the '''relationship between perspectives''' as the central unit of analysis.<ref name="dialogical">[https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1468-5914.2009.00419.x Gillespie, A. and Cornish, F. (2010), “Intersubjectivity: Towards a Dialogical Analysis,” ''Journal for the Theory of Social Behaviour'' 40(1), 19–46.]</ref>

== Primary intersubjectivity ==

Developmental psychology uses '''primary intersubjectivity''' for early dyadic coordination between infant and caregiver.

This includes reciprocal patterns such as:

* mutual attention;
* facial interaction;
* vocal timing;
* emotional coordination;
* turn-taking.

The concept is associated especially with Colwyn Trevarthen's work on early infant communication.

Such observations demonstrate structured reciprocal interaction.

They do not provide direct access to the infant's subjective experience.

== Secondary intersubjectivity ==

'''Secondary intersubjectivity''' develops when infant and caregiver coordinate attention or action toward a third object or event.

The structure becomes triadic:

<pre>
infant ↔ caregiver
   ↘       ↙
     object
</pre>

Joint attention, pointing, gaze following, and shared reference become increasingly important during the first year of life.

Developmental research commonly places robust secondary intersubjective behaviors around the later part of the first year, with major links to later language and social learning.<ref name="terrace">[https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9116197/ Terrace, H. S., Bigelow, A. E., and Beebe, B. (2022), “Intersubjectivity and the Emergence of Words.”]</ref>

== Joint attention ==

'''Joint attention''' occurs when two individuals coordinate attention toward a common referent while also tracking something about the other's attention.

Simply looking at the same object is insufficient.

Joint attention generally involves a triadic relation among:

* self;
* other;
* object or event.

Evidence can include gaze following, pointing, checking the partner's attention, or coordinated action.

Joint attention supports language learning because words can be mapped more effectively when speaker and learner share a referential focus.

== Gaze following versus joint attention ==

Gaze following is responding to the direction of another's gaze.

It can occur without full joint attention.

For stronger joint-attention claims, researchers look for evidence that the participant monitors both the object and the social partner.

This distinction matters because a reflexive orienting response is not equivalent to a mutually coordinated reference episode.

== Shared intentionality ==

Some theories use '''shared intentionality''' for coordinated activities in which participants understand goals, intentions, or commitments as jointly organized.

Examples include:

* carrying a table together;
* collaborating on a game;
* jointly solving a problem.

Shared intentionality is theoretically stronger than simultaneous behavior.

Two people can perform the same action independently without sharing a goal.

== Common ground ==

[[Common ground]] is information treated by participants as mutually available for communication.

It can include:

* shared prior conversation;
* visible surroundings;
* cultural conventions;
* known roles.

Common ground reduces the amount that must be said explicitly.

It is an operational interactional concept and does not require identical subjective experience.

== Grounding ==

Participants establish and update common ground through '''grounding'''.

A speaker contributes an utterance.

A listener may:

* acknowledge;
* ask for clarification;
* reformulate;
* correct;
* act successfully.

These responses provide evidence that the contribution has been understood sufficiently for the current purpose.

Grounding is therefore a practical mechanism of intersubjective coordination.

== Conversational repair ==

Misunderstanding is normal.

Conversation includes repair mechanisms such as:

* “Which one do you mean?”
* correction;
* repetition;
* rephrasing;
* confirmation.

Repair is theoretically important because intersubjectivity need not mean perfect shared understanding.

Instead, interaction can maintain workable understanding by identifying and correcting divergence.

== Language ==

Language supports intersubjectivity by providing public signs and conventional structures.

Speakers coordinate:

* reference;
* perspective;
* tense;
* evidentiality;
* social roles.

But linguistic meaning is not fully shared merely because the same words are used.

Participants can attach different interpretations to identical expressions.

Interaction reveals and sometimes resolves those differences.

== Intersubjectivity and theory of mind ==

[[Theory of mind]] concerns attribution of mental states such as beliefs, desires, or intentions to others.

Intersubjectivity is broader.

A person can participate in reciprocal interaction without explicitly representing a proposition such as “she believes X.”

Likewise, theory-of-mind tasks can measure mental-state reasoning without capturing the full lived relation between subjects.

The concepts overlap but should not be treated as synonyms.

== Intersubjectivity and empathy ==

Empathy can contribute to intersubjective understanding, but it is not identical to it.

Empathy concerns experiencing, recognizing, or responding to another's emotional or experiential state.

Intersubjectivity can involve shared reference, practical coordination, or discourse without emotional empathy.

== Intersubjectivity and group cognition ==

[[Group cognition]] studies cognitive processes organized through group interaction.

Intersubjectivity concerns the relations among perspectives that make some forms of group cognition possible.

A group can coordinate procedurally with limited shared understanding.

Conversely, two people can achieve deep mutual understanding without solving a collective task.

== Social norms ==

Shared social reality depends partly on norms.

Money, legal roles, rituals, and conversational conventions function because participants treat certain meanings or rules as publicly valid.

Intersubjectivity therefore connects individual experience to institutional and cultural structures.

Shared norms can also be unjust or false.

Mutual acceptance does not establish epistemic or moral correctness.

== Empirical study ==

Intersubjectivity is studied through multiple methods.

These include:

* infant observation;
* joint-attention experiments;
* referential communication tasks;
* conversation analysis;
* interaction coding;
* interviews;
* ethnography;
* motion and gaze tracking;
* physiological synchrony.

Gillespie and colleagues emphasize that different methods capture different relations among perspectives and that the relationship itself can be the appropriate unit of analysis.<ref name="dialogical" />

No single metric directly measures another person's subjective experience.

== Referential communication experiments ==

A typical experiment gives partners unfamiliar objects or figures.

One participant describes a target.

The other identifies it.

Across repeated trials, partners often develop shorter shared labels.

Researchers can measure:

* identification accuracy;
* description length;
* repair episodes;
* convergence of terms.

This operationalizes development of shared reference.

It does not demonstrate identity of subjective experience.

== Synchrony ==

Interacting people can become temporally coordinated in:

* movement;
* speech;
* gaze;
* physiological rhythms.

Synchrony can indicate coupling.

But synchrony is not sufficient for intersubjectivity.

Two metronomes can synchronize without subjective experience.

Two people can move in synchrony without understanding each other's intentions.

== Agreement and truth ==

Intersubjective agreement can stabilize knowledge claims socially.

Scientific communities use shared methods, public evidence, replication, and criticism to transform individual observations into publicly testable claims.

Yet consensus can be wrong.

Therefore:

<pre>
intersubjective agreement
≠ truth
</pre>

Truth evaluation requires evidence and appropriate epistemic standards.

== Artificial systems ==

Artificial agents can establish shared identifiers, coordinate attention, maintain common state, or repair communication.

These are operationally intersubjective-like functions.

For example, two robots can maintain a shared map.

This does not establish that either has subjective experience.

Phenomenological intersubjectivity requires subjects; whether artificial systems meet that condition is a separate question.

== Epistemic boundary ==

Different meanings of intersubjectivity should remain distinct:

'''Philosophical''' — the relation between subjects and the constitution of a shared world.

'''Developmental''' — reciprocal and triadic coordination in early social development.

'''Interactional''' — shared reference, grounding, repair, and coordination.

'''Empirical measurement''' — behavioral or physiological indicators of those processes.

Evidence for one level does not automatically establish all others.

